{
  "concept": "The plenoptic function L(x, y, θ) records the radiance arriving at every point in a scene from every direction, so a scene is fully described by a dense field of angular light distributions — and a pinhole camera is simply a device that makes that distribution sparse at points on its image plane.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal black ground line spanning the full width at the bottom",
    "Large tree on the right: bright green blobby canopy with black outline, brown trunk with two splayed roots",
    "Small black outline bird flying in the upper-right sky",
    "White/empty sky region occupying the rest of the scene",
    "Pinhole camera on the left: black rectangular outline (box) with a small gap/aperture on its right wall representing the pinhole",
    "Text label 'Pinhole camera' above the camera box",
    "Two red sample dots in free space (center of figure), one upper-right of the other",
    "Dense radial bundle of ~20 black arrows converging inward onto each free-space red dot, arrowheads at the dot, representing rays arriving from all directions",
    "Two red sample dots on the left interior wall (image plane) of the pinhole camera, one upper and one lower",
    "Exactly one arrow arriving at each in-camera red dot, each passing through the pinhole aperture, crossing each other so the upper dot receives the ray from below and the lower dot receives the ray from above (image inversion)",
    "Faint zero-intensity directional stubs around the in-camera dots indicating all other directions carry no light",
    "Side panel: polar plot of intensity L(θ) vs direction θ for the currently selected sample point",
    "Readout showing selected point coordinates (x, y) and whether it lies in free space or inside the camera",
    "Legend: red dot = sampling location, arrow = incoming ray, arrow shading/length = radiance value"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "moveSamplePoint",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "The plenoptic function is defined everywhere — clicking any point in the scene reveals the full set of ray directions and intensities arriving there, showing that the scene is a continuous field of angular light distributions rather than a set of special locations."
    },
    {
      "id": "locationMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Sample location",
      "options": ["Free space", "Inside pinhole camera"],
      "default": "Free space",
      "teaches": "In free space almost every direction carries non-zero radiance (dense star of rays), while at a point on the camera's image plane the aperture blocks nearly every direction and only one ray survives — the same function, radically different sparsity.",
      "...": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "apertureSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Pinhole aperture width",
      "range": [0.02, 1.0, 0.02],
      "default": 0.08,
      "teaches": "Widening the pinhole lets a wider cone of directions reach each image-plane point, so the angular distribution broadens and the image blurs; shrinking it isolates a single direction per pixel, which is exactly what makes a pinhole camera a clean sampler of the plenoptic function.",
      "...": ""
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Light everywhere, in every direction",
      "narration": "A red dot marks a sample point floating in open air between the camera and the tree. Arrows stream into it from every direction — green-tinted from the canopy, bright from the sky — because radiance arrives there from the whole surrounding scene.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Free space" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the point: the field is continuous",
      "narration": "Click anywhere in the sky or near the tree to relocate the sample point. The polar plot of L(θ) changes smoothly as you move, showing the plenoptic function is defined at every location, not just at cameras or surfaces.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Free space" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step inside the pinhole camera",
      "narration": "Now the sample point sits on the camera's image plane. Nearly all directions are black — the camera walls block them — and a single arrow squeezes through the pinhole. The dense star has collapsed to one spike.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "apertureSize": 0.08 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Open the aperture and blur the image",
      "narration": "Widening the pinhole admits a cone of directions at each image point, so several rays now overlap on one spot and the picture blurs. A camera image is just a 2D slice of the plenoptic function, selected by the aperture.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "apertureSize": 0.6 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Two points, an upside-down image",
      "narration": "Compare the upper and lower image-plane points: the one high on the wall receives light from low in the scene and vice versa. The crossing rays through the pinhole are why the recorded image is inverted.",
      "state": { "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera", "apertureSize": 0.08 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A camera does not create an image out of nothing — the light is already present at every point in space as the plenoptic function, and the camera merely masks away all but a few directions; a NeRF's job is to learn that full function back.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout on white with a thick black ground line at the bottom. Right third: a cartoon tree with a saturated green blobby canopy (black outline) and a brown trunk splitting into two roots; a tiny black outlined bird in the upper-right sky. Center: two red filled dots, one lower-left and one upper-right, each surrounded by a dense starburst of ~20 thin black arrows pointing inward with arrowheads touching the dot — the visual signature of 'radiance from all directions'. Left: a tall black rectangular outline labelled 'Pinhole camera' in a clean sans-serif above it, with a small gap on its right wall for the pinhole; two red dots sit on the inside of its left wall, and two long straight arrows enter through the pinhole and cross each other before terminating at the dots (upper dot fed from below, lower dot fed from above). Blocked directions at the in-camera dots should be drawn as very short faint gray stubs so the contrast between dense and sparse angular support is immediate. Add a side polar plot of L(θ) that is nearly full and bumpy for free-space points and nearly all zero with one narrow spike for in-camera points."
}

