userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using SVG.js.

CONCEPT: The plenoptic function L(x, y, θ) records the radiance arriving at every point in space from every direction, so a scene is a dense field of angular light distributions — and a pinhole camera is simply a device that makes that distribution sparse at each point on its image plane.
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): A camera does not create an image out of nothing — the light is already there at every point in space as the plenoptic function, and the camera merely masks away all directions but a few.
DEMO STEPS: 6 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

FULL BLUEPRINT:
{
  "concept": "The plenoptic function L(x, y, θ) records the radiance arriving at every point in space from every direction, so a scene is a dense field of angular light distributions — and a pinhole camera is simply a device that makes that distribution sparse at each point on its image plane.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Thick horizontal black ground line spanning the full width near the bottom",
    "Large tree filling the right third: big lumpy bright-green canopy with a heavy black outline, thick brown trunk with a forked two-root silhouette rising well above mid-height",
    "Small black outline bird flying in the upper-right sky",
    "Empty white sky filling the rest of the scene",
    "Pinhole camera on the left: tall black rectangular outline with a small gap in its right wall as the pinhole aperture",
    "Text label 'Pinhole camera' in clean sans-serif above the camera box",
    "Two large red sample dots in free space in the centre of the scene, one upper-right of the other",
    "Dense radial starburst of ~24 long black arrows converging inward onto each free-space red dot, arrowheads touching the dot, radii large enough to read at inline size",
    "Two large red sample dots on the inner left wall (image plane) of the pinhole camera, one upper and one lower",
    "Exactly one long straight arrow arriving at each in-camera red dot, each extending far to the right past the aperture toward the scene, the two rays visibly crossing at the pinhole so the upper dot is fed from below and the lower dot from above",
    "Very short faint gray stubs fanning around each in-camera dot marking directions that carry zero radiance",
    "Docked side UI panel (outside the artwork, right or bottom edge of the frame): large-titled polar 'rose' plot of L(θ) for the currently selected sample point, with generously sized title and tick text",
    "Text readout in the side panel giving the selected point's (x, y) and whether it is in free space or on the image plane"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "moveSamplePoint",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "The plenoptic function is defined everywhere — clicking any location in the scene regenerates the full star of arriving ray directions there, showing light is a continuous field rather than something that exists only at cameras or surfaces."
    },
    {
      "id": "locationMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Sample location",
      "options": [
        "Free space",
        "Inside pinhole camera"
      ],
      "default": "Free space",
      "teaches": "In free space nearly every direction carries radiance (a dense black starburst), while at a point on the camera's image plane the walls block almost everything and a single ray survives — the same function, radically different angular sparsity."
    },
    {
      "id": "apertureSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Pinhole aperture width",
      "range": [
        0.02,
        1,
        0.02
      ],
      "default": 0.08,
      "teaches": "Widening the pinhole admits a wider cone of directions at each image point, broadening the angular distribution and blurring the image; shrinking it isolates one direction per pixel, which is what makes a pinhole camera a clean point sampler of the plenoptic function."
    },
    {
      "id": "radianceTint",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Tint rays by radiance",
      "default": false,
      "teaches": "Optional colour coding shows that each arrow carries a value, not just a direction — green from the canopy, bright white-yellow from the sky — but the default black-arrow view keeps the geometry of directions the focus."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Light everywhere, in every direction",
      "narration": "Two red dots float in open air between the camera and the tree, each ringed by a dense starburst of black arrows arriving from every direction. This is the plenoptic function: radiance at a point, indexed by direction.",
      "state": {
        "locationMode": "Free space",
        "radianceTint": false
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Every arrow carries a value",
      "narration": "Turn on radiance tinting: arrows coming from the canopy go green, arrows from the open sky go bright. The direction star is really a function L(θ), plotted in the side rose panel.",
      "state": {
        "locationMode": "Free space",
        "radianceTint": true
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Move the point: the field is continuous",
      "narration": "Click anywhere in the sky or beside the tree to relocate the sample point. The side rose plot of L(θ) reshapes smoothly, showing the function is defined at every location in space.",
      "state": {
        "locationMode": "Free space",
        "radianceTint": false
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step inside the pinhole camera",
      "narration": "Now the sample points sit on the camera's image plane. Almost all directions are dead gray stubs — the walls block them — and one long ray squeezes in through the pinhole. The dense star has collapsed to a single spike.",
      "state": {
        "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera",
        "apertureSize": 0.08,
        "radianceTint": false
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Crossing rays, upside-down image",
      "narration": "Follow the two surviving rays far out to the right: the upper image point is fed from low in the scene and the lower point from high up. Crossing at the aperture is exactly why the recorded image is inverted.",
      "state": {
        "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera",
        "apertureSize": 0.08,
        "radianceTint": false
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Open the aperture and blur the image",
      "narration": "Widening the pinhole admits a cone of directions at each image point, so many rays pile onto one spot and the picture blurs. A photograph is just a thin slice of the plenoptic function, selected by the aperture.",
      "state": {
        "locationMode": "Inside pinhole camera",
        "apertureSize": 0.6,
        "radianceTint": false
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "A camera does not create an image out of nothing — the light is already there at every point in space as the plenoptic function, and the camera merely masks away all directions but a few.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape white canvas with a thick black ground line low in the frame; the default view must read as a drop-in replacement for the static textbook figure, so nothing is overlaid on the artwork. Right third: a big cartoon tree that fills nearly the full height of that third — a lumpy, irregular saturated-green canopy with a heavy black outline and a thick brown trunk that forks into two splayed roots at the ground; a small black outlined bird in the sky above it. Centre: two large red filled dots (clearly visible at inline size), each surrounded by a generous starburst of about 24 long thin BLACK arrows pointing inward with arrowheads touching the dot — black is the default; green/bright radiance tinting appears only when the tint toggle is on. Left: a tall black rectangular camera outline with the sans-serif label 'Pinhole camera' above it and a small gap in its right wall; two red dots on the inside of the left wall, each receiving one long straight arrow that continues well to the right of the aperture into the scene so the crossing geometry (upper dot fed from below, lower dot fed from above) is obvious without hovering. Blocked directions at the in-camera dots are drawn as very short faint gray stubs. The L(θ) rose plot and the (x, y) readout live in a docked side/corner UI panel OUTSIDE the drawing area, with large legible title and labels; no small legend caption inside the artwork — any key text must be large enough to read at inline scale or omitted entirely."
}

The second image above is a screenshot of the PREVIOUS attempt's rendered output (not the source figure) — use it to see exactly what broke.
